From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:51:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvk, nvk/rm: async compute queues next to the graphics queue
 (NVK_RM_ASYNC_COMPUTE)

A queue family with compute and transfer but no graphics (four queues), as
NVIDIA's driver places its compute queues: each queue's channel sits in the
TSG of the first graphics queue, on a VEID of its own, so its work runs on
the GR engine at the same time as the graphics queue's instead of being
time-sliced against it.  vkd3d-proton puts D3D12 compute and copy queues
there.  NVK_RM_ASYNC_COMPUTE=0/1 decides; on by default on GB20x.

Backend (nvkmd_rm_ctx.c): NVKMD_ENGINE_ASYNC asks for a compute-only exec
context in the device's graphics TSG (struct nvkmd_rm_tsg, refcounted; the
last context in it frees it): FERMI_CONTEXT_SHARE_A SPECIFIED with the
lowest free VEID, the GPFIFO channel with NV_CHANNEL_ALLOC_PARAMS.flags
GROUP_CHANNEL_RUNQUEUE_ONE | GROUP_CHANNEL_THREAD_ONE (GSP-RM refuses the
compute object on a VEID > 0 channel of runqueue 0), a compute object only
(no 3D on VEID > 0, no copy object on runqueue 1), and a schedule of the
channel itself (NVA06F_CTRL_CMD_GPFIFO_SCHEDULE), the TSG being scheduled
already.  A GPU wait for a value whose latest signal was submitted in the
same shared TSG does not ask for a TSG switch when it blocks: the switch
would take the signalling channel off the engine too.
NVK_RM_ASYNC_WAIT_SWITCH=1 switches on every wait.

Driver: an async compute queue runs a command buffer on its compute-only
channel when nothing in it needs another engine; the push records which
subchannels got class methods and whether MME methods were used
(nv_push::subc_used), and generated commands count as needing everything.
Other command buffers (copy-engine copies of compressed, depth or YCbCr
images, rendering clears, MME macros: compute shader invocation queries,
conditional rendering) run on a second channel of the queue with every
engine of the family, made on first use, in a TSG of its own.  The queue's
order passes from one channel to the other through a timeline: the channel
that had the latest work signals it, the other waits.  Copies, fills and
updates of buffers and of most images use NVK's compute paths there
already; query resets use a compute fill or compute report semaphores;
host methods on subchannel 0 leave the compute subchannel current.

The async family's queues may not copy depth or stencil
(VK_FORMAT_FEATURE_2_DEPTH/STENCIL_COPY_ON_COMPUTE_QUEUE_BIT_KHR are not
reported then), so ZCULL storage stays valid for depth images shared
VK_SHARING_MODE_CONCURRENT between the graphics and compute families,
which vkd3d-proton makes every image once it has two queue families.
Graphics queues are created first, so that compute queues find the TSG to
join; without one a compute queue gets a channel of its own.

The log names how many command buffers each async queue ran on which
channel when the device goes away.
